WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.250
Our rainfall report went to the

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.090
district with Riverside at over

3
00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.710
two hundred millimetres.

4
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.730
One gauge had sent nine nine

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.170
nine, its error code.

6
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.040
Nothing in our pipeline stopped it.

7
00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:16.730
Today: checks that catch problems

8
00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.250
before your report does.

9
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.230
A check is a rule the data must pass.

10
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.620
In range: rain from zero to

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.220
three hundred millimetres.

12
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:28.540
Not empty.

13
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.820
Unique: one reading per station per day.

14
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.040
Allowed values: only the stations we know.

15
00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:38.370
Some checks compare.

16
00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.790
Do the class counts add up

17
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:41.670
to the school's roll?

18
00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.140
Did today's batch arrive at all?

19
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.360
Is the date this week, and not last year?

20
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.930
In SQL, write each check as a

21
00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.890
query that finds the bad rows.

22
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.200
If it returns nothing, the check passes.

23
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.600
If it returns rows, those are your

24
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.080
problems, with their details.

25
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.200
Then decide what a failure does.

26
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.750
Stop the batch, and lose

27
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.050
good data with the bad.

28
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.810
Set the bad rows aside, and

29
00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:12.610
let the rest through.

30
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.740
Or warn, and let it all through.

31
00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.650
Choose by what a wrong report costs.

32
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.080
And fix problems at their source.

33
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.780
Send the reading back to the club that

34
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.100
took it, so tomorrow's batch is right.

35
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.740
Cleaning the same mistake every

36
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.880
week means nobody fixed it.

37
00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:33.630
Quick check!

38
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.000
Which check would have

39
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.500
caught the nine nine nine?

40
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:40.300
In range.

41
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.130
Nine nine nine is far above

42
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.050
three hundred millimetres, so

43
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.410
the range check returns it.

44
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.680
Not empty and unique would

45
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.660
both have let it through.

46
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.380
But checks only catch what you thought of.

47
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.170
A reading of twelve, when it really

48
00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.810
rained twenty, passes every check.

49
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.040
So also compare with

50
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.040
neighbours, and look at the chart.

51
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.870
In the app, turn the checks on and off,

52
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.890
and choose what a failure does.

53
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.790
With “Stop the batch”, only the

54
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:13.890
first day gets through.

55
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.790
Without the repeats check,

56
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.610
Hilltop's average creeps up.

57
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:19.330
Your turn.

58
00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.920
Every check passes on today's batch.

59
00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.360
What can I say about the data?

60
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.250
No known problem was found; that's all.

61
00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.170
Remember: write checks for range,

62
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.150
gaps, repeats and allowed values.

63
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.570
Decide what each failure does, and

64
00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:38.970
fix problems at their source.

65
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.900
And a passing check is not

66
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.320
proof the data is right.

67
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.880
Try it in class tomorrow: ask your pupils

68
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.080
to write checks for their own class data.

69
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.330
A mark must be from zero to twenty.

70
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.730
Every pupil appears once.

71
00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:57.610
Then test the checks on a made-up

72
00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.930
sheet with planted mistakes.
